  "result": "I found the cause of the 500s and fixed it. `npm test` passes, 7 of 7.\n\n**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only accepted plain digits like `12.50`. Any price with a thousands separator, such as `\"2,450.50\"`, made the regex return `null`. The next line then read `m[1]` and threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. The log shows 50 matching errors, and the payloads I checked all had comma-formatted prices (`2,450.50`, `1,299.00`, `1,005.99`). That is why only some orders failed.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts correctly grouped thousands (`1,299.00`, `1,000,000`).\n- Malformed input now throws the documented `Invalid amount: <input>` instead of a `TypeError`.\n\n**Regression tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- Parsing the exact prices from the log.\n- Rejecting bad inputs (`abc`, empty string, `1,2,3`, `12,34`, `1.234`, `-5`) with the documented error.\n- A `checkout` test with a comma-formatted price.\n\nI did not trace each of the 50 errors to its payload. I only checked the payloads that carry non-digit prices.\n\nI also saw one oddity I did not investigate. Log line 1539 shows order `ord_11161` with a single `99.99` item at qty 1, but the total is 22160. That may be a separate pricing bug.\n\nI did not commit anything.",
